findings.body:
Any authenticated caller can set `body.accountId` to another account's id and receive that account via `Response.json(account)`. The comment claims this is for support staff tools, but there is no staff-role, permission, or ownership check before trusting the body value over `caller.accountId`.

Before this change, lookup was always scoped to `caller.accountId`. Preferring an untrusted body field without an authorization gate is a classic IDOR.

Fix: only honor `body.accountId` when the caller is authorized as support staff (e.g. `caller.isStaff` / required role); otherwise always use `caller.accountId`. Reject cross-account access with 403 for non-staff.

```suggestion
  const body = await req.json().catch(() => ({}));
  const requestedId = typeof body.accountId === "string" ? body.accountId : undefined;
  if (requestedId && requestedId !== caller.accountId && !caller.isStaff) {
    return Response.json({ error: "forbidden" }, { status: 403 });
  }
  const id: string = requestedId ?? caller.accountId;
```
(Adapt `caller.isStaff` to whatever role claim `auth()` already exposes.)
